From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 00:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] stm32/sdio: Invalidate the D-cache after a DMA read.

sdio_transfer_cmd53() cleans and invalidates the receive buffer's cache
lines before a DMA read, so that nothing dirty is written back over what
the DMA writes. It did nothing once the transfer had finished. A Cortex-M7
can fill a line of the buffer again while the DMA is still writing to RAM
(a speculative read can do it), and the CPU then reads that line's old
contents instead of the new data.

On an Arduino GIGA R1 (STM32H747) this showed up as firmware builds
that joined Wi-Fi but passed no traffic: the CYW43 driver read a stale
first line of the buffer it receives packets into, and DHCP never
completed. Whether a build failed depended only on how its code was
laid out, and an affected image failed on every boot. On such an image,
making that one 32-byte line non-cacheable through the MPU was enough
for DHCP, DNS and TCP to work, while the same MPU region left cacheable
still failed. The same image with an invalidate added after the DMA
read got an address on each of two boots, and without it failed on both.

Clean and invalidate the buffer again after a DMA read completes, as
dma_unprotect_rx_region() does for SPI and the SD card. Reads that don't
use DMA, and writes, are unchanged.

diff --git a/ports/stm32/sdio.c b/ports/stm32/sdio.c
--- a/ports/stm32/sdio.c
+++ b/ports/stm32/sdio.c
@@ -515,6 +515,13 @@
         #if defined(STM32F7)
         dma_nohal_deinit(&dma_SDIO_0);
         #endif
+        if (!write) {
+            // The cache was cleaned and invalidated before the transfer, but the
+            // CPU can fill lines of buf again while the DMA is writing to RAM (a
+            // Cortex-M7 may read speculatively), and those lines then hold the
+            // old data. Invalidate again so the CPU reads what the DMA wrote.
+            MP_HAL_CLEANINVALIDATE_DCACHE(buf, len);
+        }
     }
 
     return 0;
